statement:
`fruits` is an `n x n` grid of rooms, and `fruits[i][j]` is the number of fruits lying in room `(i, j)`. Three children start in the corner rooms `(0, 0)`, `(0, n - 1)` and `(n - 1, 0)`. Each child makes exactly `n - 1` moves and has to finish in room `(n - 1, n - 1)`. A move must stay inside the grid, and from room `(i, j)`:

- the child from `(0, 0)` steps to `(i + 1, j + 1)`, `(i + 1, j)` or `(i, j + 1)`;
- the child from `(0, n - 1)` steps to `(i + 1, j - 1)`, `(i + 1, j)` or `(i + 1, j + 1)`;
- the child from `(n - 1, 0)` steps to `(i - 1, j + 1)`, `(i, j + 1)` or `(i + 1, j + 1)`.

A child picks up all the fruits of every room it is in, its starting room included. A room that several children pass through gives up its fruits only once. Return the largest total number of fruits the three children can gather together.

validator:
function validate([fruits]) {
  const n = fruits.length;
  if (n < 2 || n > 1000) return 'fruits needs 2 to 1000 rows.';
  if (fruits.some(row => row.length !== n)) return 'fruits must be square: every row needs n values.';
  if (fruits.some(row => row.some(v => v < 0 || v > 1000))) return 'Each room holds 0 to 1000 fruits.';
  return null;
}

